  "query_preview": "..\nToday and Thursday: The region will be under a deepening upper level \ntrough. A weak area of low pressure will settle over northern WA, \nalong the Canadian border, and bring increased precipitable water \nand instability. Precipitable water amount will be near an inch. \nCape values will range from 200-500 J/kg for areas north of Highway \n2 in WA and the Idaho Panhandle. Ensembles have thunderstorm chances \nstarting around 11 AM and lasting into the evening. Best chances are \nnorth of the Highway 2 corridor with probabilities of 20-40%. Th \nrest of the region has less than 20%. Main concerns with the storms \nwill be new fire starts and gusty 35+ mph outflow winds impacting \nongoing and new fires. Brief periods of heavy rain could bring \nlocalized debris flows over burn scars. Best potential is the North \nCascades with 0.2-0.5 of an inch. Confidence is low on cells moving \nover these areas. Winds will increase from the north later Wednesday \ndown the Okanogan Valley to western Basin, creating a more generic \nelevated fire weather conditions. Showers and thunderstorms will \ncalm overnight but return again late Thursday morning. Probability \nis weaker on Thursday with 15-30%. \n\nFriday through Wednesday: Friday will have some lingering mountain \nshowers as the trough is pushed out of the region by a strengthening \nridge. The region will be on the up slope side of the ridge, \nallowing for warmer, less dry air to infiltrate the region. \nUnfortunately, the pattern is not bringing widespread beneficial \nprecip to the region. Models are only giving brief periods of \nafternoon showers mainly over the higher terrains. The amounts are \nnot expected to be more than a tenth. Highs will continue to be in \nthe upper 80s and 90s. Overnight lows will be in the upper 40s to \nlow 60s. /JDC",
